{
  "id": "adjektive-mit-praepositionen",
  "name": "Adjectives + prepositions",
  "level": "B2",
  "status": "geprueft",
  "subtitle": "Many adjectives need a fixed preposition: good at, interested in, afraid of, proud of. After the preposition use a noun or the -ing form: good at cooking.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "good at",
    "interested in",
    "afraid of",
    "proud of",
    "-ing"
  ],
  "source": "EnglishAcademy original English text (grammar-b2 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "be + adjective",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "+ fixed preposition",
        "variant": "prep"
      },
      {
        "text": "+ noun or -ing"
      },
      {
        "text": "angry with sb / about sth"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "I'm <span class=\"vp-verb\">proud</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">of</span> my daughter. — Amira is <span class=\"vp-verb\">good</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">at</span> fixing bikes. — Who is she <span class=\"vp-verb\">married</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">to</span>?",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "Many adjectives are followed by a <strong>fixed preposition</strong>. Learn the two words together, like one word: <em>good at, interested in, afraid of, proud of, keen on, fond of, responsible for, similar to, married to, worried about</em>.",
        "tag": "Fixed pairs"
      },
      {
        "html": "After a preposition you use a <strong>noun</strong> or the <strong>-ing form</strong>, never the infinitive: <em>She is afraid of flying.</em> <em>Tom is good at cooking.</em> <em>I'm tired of waiting.</em> For a whole idea you can also use a noun: <em>I'm proud of my sister.</em>",
        "tag": "Noun or -ing"
      },
      {
        "html": "Some adjectives change the preposition with the meaning. <strong>angry with</strong> a person, <strong>angry about</strong> a thing: <em>I'm angry with Tom. I'm angry about the delay.</em> <strong>different from</strong> (also <em>different to</em> in British English): <em>Her flat is different from mine.</em> American English often says <em>different than</em>.",
        "tag": "Two prepositions"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>I'm interested on music.</em> → ✓ <em>I'm interested in music.</em> · ✗ <em>She is married with a doctor.</em> → ✓ <em>She is married to a doctor.</em> · ✗ <em>He is good at to cook.</em> → ✓ <em>He is good at cooking.</em> (a verb after a preposition takes -ing)",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "In a question the preposition usually stays at the end: <em>What are you afraid of? Who is she married to?</em> (see <a href=\"/da-wo-woerter\">prepositions at the end</a>). The verb often takes the same preposition as the adjective: <em>I'm worried about the exam</em> = <em>I worry about the exam</em>. Many verbs also have fixed prepositions: <a href=\"/verben-praepositionen\">Verbs + prepositions</a>. For the <em>-ing</em> form after a preposition, see <a href=\"/verben-als-nomen\">Gerunds</a>. Note: <em>good at</em> is about skill (<em>good at maths</em>); <em>good with</em> is about handling people or things (<em>good with children</em>).",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show overview ▼",
      "hideLabel": "Hide overview ▲",
      "tables": [
        {
          "caption": "at, in, of, on",
          "head": [
